# Zod Schema Integration

## Core Principles
1. Use Zod schemas as the single source of truth
2. Map Zod types to DynamoDB types
3. Support all DynamoDB Toolbox features through Zod

## Schema Definition
```typescript
import { z } from 'zod';
import { createEntity } from '../dynojs';

// Define base schema for all entities
const baseSchema = z.object({
  pk: z.string(),
  sk: z.string(),
  type: z.string(),
  createdAt: z.date(),
  updatedAt: z.date()
});

// Define user schema extending base
const userSchema = baseSchema.extend({
  type: z.literal('USER'),
  name: z.string(),
  email: z.string().email(),
  age: z.number().optional(),
  tags: z.set(z.string()).optional(),
  metadata: z.record(z.any()).optional()
});

// Create entity using Zod schema
const UserEntity = createEntity({
  name: 'users',
  schema: userSchema,
  // Define indexes using schema fields
  indexes: {
    email: {
      pk: 'email',
      sk: 'type'
    },
    age: {
      pk: 'pk',
      sk: 'age'
    }
  }
});
```

## DynamoDB Type Mapping
```typescript
// Scalar Types
const scalarSchema = z.object({
  // String (S)
  name: z.string(),
  
  // Number (N)
  age: z.number(),
  
  // Binary (B)
  data: z.instanceof(Buffer),
  
  // Boolean (BOOL)
  isActive: z.boolean(),
  
  // Null (NULL)
  optionalField: z.null().optional()
});

// Set Types
const setSchema = z.object({
  // String Set (SS)
  tags: z.set(z.string()),
  
  // Number Set (NS)
  scores: z.set(z.number()),
  
  // Binary Set (BS)
  dataSets: z.set(z.instanceof(Buffer))
});

// Document Types
const documentSchema = z.object({
  // Map (M)
  metadata: z.record(z.any()),
  
  // List (L)
  items: z.array(z.any())
});
```

## Complex Schema Patterns
```typescript
// Polymorphic Types
const itemSchema = z.discriminatedUnion('type', [
  z.object({
    type: z.literal('USER'),
    name: z.string(),
    email: z.string()
  }),
  z.object({
    type: z.literal('ORDER'),
    orderId: z.string(),
    amount: z.number()
  })
]);

// Nested Objects
const nestedSchema = z.object({
  address: z.object({
    street: z.string(),
    city: z.string(),
    country: z.string()
  }),
  preferences: z.object({
    theme: z.string(),
    notifications: z.boolean()
  })
});

// Computed Fields
const computedSchema = z.object({
  firstName: z.string(),
  lastName: z.string(),
  fullName: z.string().transform(
    (val, ctx) => `${ctx.parent.firstName} ${ctx.parent.lastName}`
  )
});
```

## Index Definitions
```typescript
// Define indexes using schema fields
const UserEntity = createEntity({
  name: 'users',
  schema: userSchema,
  indexes: {
    // GSI
    email: {
      pk: 'email',
      sk: 'type'
    },
    // LSI
    age: {
      pk: 'pk',
      sk: 'age'
    }
  }
});
```

## Validation and Type Safety
```typescript
// Runtime validation
const result = await UserEntity.put({
  pk: 'USER#123',
  sk: 'METADATA',
  type: 'USER',
  name: 'John',
  email: 'invalid-email' // Will fail validation
});

// Type inference
type User = z.infer<typeof userSchema>;
const user: User = {
  pk: 'USER#123',
  sk: 'METADATA',
  type: 'USER',
  name: 'John',
  email: 'john@example.com'
};
```

## Limitations and Considerations
1. Schema Complexity
   - Zod can handle most DynamoDB data types
   - Complex nested structures may need custom transformers
   - Some DynamoDB-specific features may need additional configuration

2. Index Management
   - Index definitions still need to be specified separately
   - Schema fields used in indexes must be properly typed
   - Composite keys may need custom validation

3. Performance
   - Complex Zod schemas may impact validation performance
   - Consider using `.strict()` for better type inference
   - Use `.passthrough()` for flexible schemas

## Best Practices
1. Schema Organization
   - Use base schemas for common fields
   - Extend schemas for specific entities
   - Keep schemas modular and reusable

2. Type Safety
   - Use strict mode for better type inference
   - Define proper types for all fields
   - Use discriminated unions for polymorphic types

3. Validation
   - Add custom validators for business rules
   - Use transformers for computed fields
   - Handle edge cases explicitly
